  "codebook": {
    "definition": "Credit-claiming occurs when a senator explicitly or implicitly takes personal responsibility for securing a benefit, funding, policy outcome, or other positive result for their constituents, state, or a specific group. This includes highlighting their direct involvement in legislative, funding, or advocacy efforts that led to the outcome.",
    "clarification": "Credit-claiming is identified when the senator uses first-person language (e.g., 'I secured,' 'I fought for,' 'I introduced') or explicitly ties a positive outcome to their personal actions, committee membership, or advocacy. It can also include external recognition of the senator's efforts (e.g., awards, named legislation) that the senator acknowledges.",
    "positive_examples": [
      {
        "document_id": "89",
        "explanation": "Senator Dorgan explicitly states that he authored legislation creating the National Center designation for Bismarck State College and that he will present the designation. This is a direct claim of personal responsibility for the outcome."
      },
